+// Where a dual vertex goes with Keep sharp edges: the point that sits best on the
+// planes through its patch's crossings, each square to its face's normal (the quadric
+// error minimum of dual contouring), when those normals spread across more than one
+// direction (a crease, or a corner with three), solved about the crossings' mean along
+// the strong axes only, so a crease's vertex stays free along its line; and when that
+// point lies in the cell (a little beyond it, for a crease on its face). Elsewhere, or
+// where a crossing has no face (a kept thin part's own), the mean. Whether it is sharp.
+@(private = "file")
+dual_sharp_point :: proc(r: ^Remesher, edge: [12]i32, loop: []u8, mean: Vec3, i: i32, j, k: int) -> (at: Vec3, sharp: bool) {
+	a: matrix[3, 3]f32
+	b: Vec3
+	for e in loop {
+		v := edge[e]
+		n := r.cross_n[v]
+		if n == {} do return mean, false
+		p := r.out.positions[v]
+		a += linalg.outer_product(n, n)
+		b += n * linalg.dot(n, p)
+	}
+	values, vectors := symmetric_eigen3(a)
+	if !(values[0] > 0) || values[1] < SHARP_SPREAD * values[0] do return mean, false
+	rest := b - a * mean
